         _desc = "Horizontal map scroller using dual screen buffers at $0400 and $0C00. JSR Scroller_L each frame to scroll left, JSR Scroller_R to scroll right. Fine scroll via $D016 bits 0-2, coarse step loads one new column from MAP_DATA and flips $D018. Colour mode 0=none, 1=deferred, 2=inline. Combine with MACRO_VSCROLL by JSRing both entry points per frame.";
     } else if (_node.node_type == "MACRO_METASCROLL") {
-        _desc = "Four-way pixel-smooth camera over a META_TILESET room. The room is flattened at compile time into a char plane and a colour plane, so the runtime never decodes stamps. JSR MSC_L/MSC_R/MSC_U/MSC_D to move the camera one pixel that way, and JSR MSC_Update every frame so a coarse step still finishes when nothing is moving. Clicking any of those five names on the node drops a ready-made JSR node for it. Fine scroll is $D016 for L/R and $D011 for U/D. The window is cols 0-38 and rows 0-24 - the outer cells scroll too, because a new column or row has to be loaded into a cell the border is covering or it appears at full width the instant it is written. 38-column mode covers 7 pixels on the left and 9 on the right, 24-row mode 4 top and 4 bottom, so col 38 and row 24 reveal perfectly from 0 to 7 pixels, while col 0 and row 0 leave a single pixel of pop - the left cover is 7 pixels and a character is 8, so that last pixel is a hardware limit; cover it with a sprite if it shows. Col 39 is the only pad and is invisible at every fine-scroll value. BLANK CH is the character the screen is cleared to at init and the one left in col 39; it defaults to char 0 rather than $20, because $20 is a space only in the ROM charset and is usually a real glyph in a custom one. COLOUR MODE decides what happens to colour RAM. FIXED is the stock C64 setting: in multicolour text mode the three shared colours come from $D021/$D022/$D023 and the colour-RAM nibble only supplies the fourth, so a room drawn from one palette needs no colour scrolling at all - the nibble is written once at init, the coarse step costs a single frame and the colour plane is never built. SHIFT scrolls colour RAM per cell for maps that really do vary, splitting the coarse step over two frames - chars first, then colour - which is worth it on a C64 Ultimate in turbo but not on stock hardware.";
+        _desc = "Four-way pixel-smooth camera over a META_TILESET room. The room is flattened at compile time into a char plane and a colour plane, so the runtime never decodes stamps. JSR MSC_L/MSC_R/MSC_U/MSC_D to move the camera one pixel that way, and JSR MSC_Update every frame so a coarse step still finishes when nothing is moving. Clicking any of those five names on the node drops a ready-made JSR node for it. Fine scroll is $D016 for L/R and $D011 for U/D. The window is cols 0-38 and rows 0-24 - the outer cells scroll too, because a new column or row has to be loaded into a cell the border is covering or it appears at full width the instant it is written. 38-column mode covers 7 pixels on the left and 9 on the right, 24-row mode 4 top and 4 bottom, so col 38 and row 24 reveal perfectly from 0 to 7 pixels, while col 0 and row 0 leave a single pixel of pop - the left cover is 7 pixels and a character is 8, so that last pixel is a hardware limit; cover it with a sprite if it shows. Col 39 is the only pad and is invisible at every fine-scroll value. BLANK CH is the character the screen is cleared to at init and the one left in col 39; it defaults to char 0 rather than $20, because $20 is a space only in the ROM charset and is usually a real glyph in a custom one. COLOUR MODE decides what happens to colour RAM. FIXED is the stock C64 setting: in multicolour text mode the three shared colours come from $D021/$D022/$D023 and the colour-RAM nibble only supplies the fourth, so a room drawn from one palette needs no colour scrolling at all - the nibble is written once at init, the coarse step costs a single frame and the colour plane is never built. SHIFT scrolls colour RAM per cell for maps that really do vary, splitting the coarse step over two frames - chars first, then colour. That split is sequencing rather than cycles, so the one-frame colour lag it causes shows on a fast machine too. SHIFT C64U does both passes in the same frame and removes the lag, at roughly twice the cost - about 80 percent of a PAL frame, which fits inside a frame at 1 MHz but runs down the visible display, so it is clean on a C64 Ultimate in turbo and tears on stock hardware.";
     } else if (_node.node_type == "MACRO_VSCROLL") {
